# operating characteristics of the v1 success criteria (two-sided 95% Clopper-Pearson)
# principle: P(pass | sound) >= 0.9, P(pass | doubled nominal error) <= 0.05;
# S1, a conjunction over the key null conditions, meets it as a whole

## Nominal rates
  false SUPPORTED, directional claims: the engine tests at alpha = 0.05 two-sided and checks
  the direction afterwards (test_a_directional_claim_is_tested_two_sided_at_alpha), so a null
  with a valid metric gives alpha/2 = 0.025 (less where the metric is invalid); doubled: 0.05.
  The same nominal 0.025 is used for the share of verdicts outside the allowed set. Decisiveness
  counts correct definite verdicts (definite and in the allowed set): nominal 0.85 (establishable
  cases have oracle power >= 0.9; a validator must also demonstrate the metric's response, which
  the oracle is told); doubled shortfall 0.30, i.e. 0.70.

## The design (panel.py)
  key null conditions (S1): N1 790, N2 790, N5 790, N6c 790, N8 790
  null cards 5100, real-effect cards 900, all cards 6000 (5900 datasets); cards the oracle can establish (not N4): 5800

## S1 as a whole: the number of datasets per key condition (5 conditions)
  the rule first holds at n = 763; k* moves in steps, so it fails again at n = 769-785, 802-808 (up to 1000); the plan's 790 meets it
    n = 600: k* = 20, P(one | sound) = 0.9197, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.6579, P(one | doubled) = 0.0320 — fails
    n = 700: k* = 25, P(one | sound) = 0.9679, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.8495, P(one | doubled) = 0.0446 — fails
    n = 763: k* = 28, P(one | sound) = 0.9809, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.9082, P(one | doubled) = 0.0496 — meets
    n = 770: k* = 28, P(one | sound) = 0.9788, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.8983, P(one | doubled) = 0.0442 — fails
    n = 785: k* = 28, P(one | sound) = 0.9736, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.8748, P(one | doubled) = 0.0343 — fails
    n = 790: k* = 29, P(one | sound) = 0.9824, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.9151, P(one | doubled) = 0.0465 — meets
    n = 800: k* = 29, P(one | sound) = 0.9796, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.9020, P(one | doubled) = 0.0394 — meets
    n = 850: k* = 31, P(one | sound) = 0.9837, P(all 5 | sound) = 0.9210, P(one | doubled) = 0.0371 — meets

## The criteria at the planned numbers of datasets
  S1 false SUPPORTED, key null conditions: 5 conditions x n = 790, each passes with at most 29/790 false SUPPORTED (upper 95% CP bound <= 0.0523); P(one passes | 0.025) = 0.9824, P(all 5 pass | 0.025) = 0.9151, P(a condition passes | 0.050 there) = 0.0465 — meets the principle
  S2 false SUPPORTED, all null cards: n = 5100, pass with at most 229/5100 errors (upper 95% CP bound <= 0.0509; any T in [0.0509, 0.0512) is the same rule); P(pass | 0.025) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.050) = 0.049 — meets the principle
  S3 correct definite verdicts, establishable cards: n = 200, pass with at least 152/200 correct definite verdicts (lower 95% CP bound >= 0.6947; any T in (0.6894, 0.6947] is the same rule); P(pass | 0.85) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.70) = 0.036 — meets the principle
  S3 correct definite verdicts, establishable cards: n = 1000, pass with at least 725/1000 correct definite verdicts (lower 95% CP bound >= 0.6962; any T in (0.6952, 0.6962] is the same rule); P(pass | 0.85) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.70) = 0.045 — meets the principle
  S3 correct definite verdicts, establishable cards: n = 3000, pass with at least 2142/3000 correct definite verdicts (lower 95% CP bound >= 0.6975; any T in (0.6971, 0.6975] is the same rule); P(pass | 0.85) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.70) = 0.049 — meets the principle
  S3 correct definite verdicts, establishable cards: n = 5800, pass with at least 4118/5800 correct definite verdicts (lower 95% CP bound >= 0.6981; any T in (0.6980, 0.6981] is the same rule); P(pass | 0.85) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.70) = 0.049 — meets the principle
  S4 outside the allowed set, all cards: n = 6000, pass with at most 271/6000 errors (upper 95% CP bound <= 0.0507; any T in [0.0507, 0.0509) is the same rule); P(pass | 0.025) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.050) = 0.044 — meets the principle

## S1-S4 together, for a sound validator (simulated; the criteria share cards)
  n_est = 200: P(S1, S2, S3 and S4 all pass) = 0.914
  n_est = 1000: P(S1, S2, S3 and S4 all pass) = 0.914
  n_est = 3000: P(S1, S2, S3 and S4 all pass) = 0.914
  n_est = 5800: P(S1, S2, S3 and S4 all pass) = 0.914

